            <ArticleTitle>Lumbar injuries of the pediatric population.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>289-311</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pop.2013.02.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0095-4543(13)00026-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Primary musculoskeletal etiologies, such as stress fractures, strains, facet arthropathy, and sacroiliac joint dysfunction, are more common causes of pediatric pain than systemic diseases, both in the office setting and the emergency room. Systemic features, young age, and atypical pain should clue physicians to causes other than a primary musculoskeletal cause and prompt an intensive search for other conditions.</AbstractText>
